  {
    "episode_id": 89059,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89059.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 28,
    "turn_text": "I was going to say for our younger listeners, Harry Reid, well known for a lot of, he was majority leader of the Senate, so very powerful man, but I think in historical terms will be remembered for probably his role in Obamacare, probably the UFO issue as a quirk, but also if you go back to, I think it was like 2006, he was the first real political figure who encouraged Barack Obama to run for president, which obviously changed the course of history in a lot of different ways. So shifting from your national experience to the state experience, I'm gonna ask a selfish question here as a candidate for the state legislature. You rose up really quickly in house leadership. You basically were appointed to the office and then almost immediately we're chairing a major policy committee and the healthcare committee. You were tapped to be chair of redistricting, which is one of the most challenging committees. I think you were a majority whip as well. So what's the secret sauce for how you were able to so quickly assume those large responsibilities in the state legislature?",
