  {
    "episode_id": 89059,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/9/89059.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 42,
    "turn_text": "Well, I think we're starting to see the challenges of it right now, right, with states putting restrictions on abortion access. And we have already felt it. I know from our Planned Parenthood, clinics to our private clinics, we are going to see people, you know, Idaho, it's not like, I think the, the need for abortion and reproductive health care is not going to suddenly go away, right. Women still make up 50% of the population, you know, according to the Guttmacher Institute, I think their last survey in 2020 or 2021 70%. of Americans still feel like the right to abortion is the right thing. So that's a big percentage, right? So I think even in these states where you're seeing really tight restrictions on abortion access, the need for abortion is not going to go away and safe. I would hope it would be safe and legal where people sought their abortion care, but that's going to mean the states that are still standing to protect the right to abortion. And so I think what I was about to say is we're already seeing an influx of patients here in Oregon, and I think, you know, other states that keep it legal and safe will have the same challenges. And so I think being able to help women pay for and access the abortion service that they're seeking is going to be a challenge for states. Because, you know, a handful of, you know, 20 states can't take on the other 30 states abortion care needs. Totally.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_00",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "We are currently experiencing challenges related to abortion access.",
